            <ArticleTitle>Clonazepam for the treatment of lancinating phantom limb pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>59-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To propose clonazepam for use in the empiric treatment of shooting/shocking phantom limb pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient pain clinic associated with a university hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two patients with phantom limb pain after total hip disarticulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Treatment with clonazepam.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Clonazepam provided effective relief for &gt; 6 months in two patients with shooting/shocking phantom limb pain. Although clonazepam therapy is not new, it appears to have been omitted from current pain texts and journals as a treatment option for phantom limb pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
